This is a post about topology and about groups.
While theoretically it is possible and in scope
for me to introduce topology from the bottom up,
I suspect that you won't get anything out of the story
that I want to tell if it really is your first time
seeing the definition of a topology, so I won't.
However, if you know topology and groups separately,
this is a perfect place to meet topological groups,
since I'm going to talk only about pretty elementary things.

# Topological Groups

A _topological group_ is a group [`G`]($mathtex)
which is at the same time a topological space
so that multiplication [`(g,h) \mapsto g h`]($mathtex)
and inversion [`g \mapsto g^{-1}`]($mathtex)
are continuous as maps [`G \times G \to G`]($mathtex)
and [`G \to G`]($mathtex) respectively.

An _identity neighborhood_ is a an open subset of a topological group
containing the identity, [`1`]($mathtex).
Identity neighborhoods satisfy the following axioms.
+ If [`U, V`]($mathtex) are identity neighborhoods, so is [`U \cap V`]($mathtex).
+ If [`U`]($mathtex) is an identity neighborhood, so is [`g U g^{-1}`]($mathtex)
  and [`U^{-1}`]($mathtex) for every [`g \in G`]($mathtex).
+ If [`U`]($mathtex) is an identity neighborhood, there is another identity neighborhood
  [`V`]($mathtex) such that [`V^2 \subset U`]($mathtex).

To clarify notation, [`g U g^{-1} = \{ g u g^{-1} : u \in U \}`]($mathtex),
[`U^{-1} = \{ u^{-1} : u \in U \}`]($mathtex),
and [`V^2 = \{ u v : u, v \in U \}`]($mathtex).
The second bullet point follows from continuity of the map
[`x \mapsto (g,x) \mapsto g x \mapsto (g x, g^{-1}) \mapsto g x g^{-1}`]($mathtex)
and inversion, respectively,
and the third bullet point follows from continuity of multiplication.

A slight weakening of these axioms suffice to characterize group topologies.

> **Proposition 1.**
> Suppose that [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex) is a collection of subsets of a group [`G`]($mathtex)
> satisfying the following axioms.
> + We have [`1 \in U`]($mathtex) for all [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex).
> + For each [`U, V \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex), there exists [`W \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
>   satisfying [`W \subset U \cap V`]($mathtex).
> + For each [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex), there exists [`V \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
>   satisfying [`V^2 \subset U`]($mathtex).
> + For each [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex), there exists [`V \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
>   satisfying [`V \subset U^{-1}`]($mathtex).
> + For each [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex) and [`g \in G`]($mathtex), there exists [`V \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
>   satisfying [`V \subset g U g^{-1}`]($mathtex).
>
> Then there is a group topology on [`G`]($mathtex) where a subset [`S`]($mathtex) is open
> if and only if for each [`g \in S`]($mathtex) there exists [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
> such that [`g U \subset S`]($mathtex).

*Proof.*
The claimed topology really is a topology:
the defining property of open sets is clearly closed under arbitrary unions.
If [`g \in g U \subset S`]($mathtex) and [`g \in g V \subset T`]($mathtex),
then by assumption there exists [`W \subset U \cap V`]($mathtex)
so that [`g W \subset S \cap T`]($mathtex),
so finite intersections of open sets are open.

Suppose [`g h \in S`]($mathtex) where [`S`]($mathtex) is open.
To show that multiplication is continuous, we show that
there are sets [`U, V \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
so that [`g U h V \subset S`]($mathtex).
Indeed, since [`S`]($mathtex) is open,
it contains [`g h W`]($mathtex) for [`W \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex),
and we choose [`U, V \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex)
so that [`V^2 \subset W`]($mathtex)
and [`U \subset h V h^{-1}`]($mathtex).
We compute:
```=mathtex
g U h V \subset g h V h^{-1} h V = g h V^2 \subset g h W
```
as required.

To show that inversion is continuous,
given [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex),
take [`V \subset g U g^{-1}`]($mathtex)
and [`W \subset V^{-1}`]($mathtex).
Then
```=mathtex
g^{-1}W \subset g^{-1} V^{-1} \subset g^{-1} g U^{-1} g^{-1} = U^{-1} g^{-1} = {(g U)}^{-1}.
```
Thus this topology makes [`G`]($mathtex) a topological group. [`\QED`]($mathtex)

# Groups acting on graphs

Let [`G`]($mathtex) be a group acting on a connected, simplicial graph [`\Gamma`]($mathtex)
by graph automorphisms without inversions in edges.
_Simplicial_ means that between two vertices of [`\Gamma`]($mathtex), there is at most one edge,
and that no edge forms a loop.
_Without inversions in eges_ means that if an edge of [`\Gamma`]($mathtex)
is fixed setwise by a group element, it is fixed pointwise.
A consequence of this latter condition is that the stabilizers of an edge [`e`]($mathtex)
incident to the vertices [`v`]($mathtex) and [`w`]($mathtex)
is naturally a subgroup of the stabilizers of both [`v`]($mathtex) and [`w`]($mathtex).
Another is that the quotient map [`\Gamma \to G \backslash \Gamma`]($mathtex) is a graph map.

> **Proposition 2.**
> Suppose [`G`]($mathtex) acts on a connected, simplicial graph [`\Gamma`]($mathtex)
> by graph automorphisms without inversions in edges,
> and that for each vertex [`v \in \Gamma`]($mathtex),
> the stabilizer [`G_v`]($mathtex) is equipped with a group topology
> such that the following conditions hold.
> + For each [`g \in G`]($mathtex), conjugation by [`g`]($mathtex)
>   induces a homeomorphism [`G_v \to g G_v g^{-1} = G_{g.v}`]($mathtex).
> + If [`e`]($mathtex) is an edge of [`\Gamma`]($mathtex)
>   incident to vertices [`v`]($mathtex) and [`w`]($mathtex),
>   the inclusion of the stabilizer [`G_e`]($mathtex) into [`G_v`]($mathtex)
>   and [`G_w`]($mathtex) yields the same topology on [`G_e`]($mathtex),
>   such that both inclusions are open embeddings.
>
> Then the family [`\mathcal{B} = \{ U \subset G_v : U \text{ is an identity neighborhood } \}`]($mathtex)
> satisfies the hypotheses of Proposition 1.
> The induced topology on [`G`]($mathtex) is the finest group topology on [`G`]($mathtex)
> such that each inclusion [`G_v \to G`]($mathtex) is an open embedding.
> In this topology the group [`G`]($mathtex) acts continuously on [`\Gamma`]($mathtex).

_Proof._
We verify the hypotheses of **Proposition 1.**
The first, that each [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex) contains the identity, is clear,
as are the third and fourth, since each [`G_v`]($mathtex) is a topological group.
The final hypothesis, about conjugation, holds by definition.
Thus we need only verify that [`U \cap V`]($mathtex) contains an element of [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex).
Supposing that [`U \subset G_u`]($mathtex) and [`V \subset G_v`]($mathtex)
for vertices [`u, v \in \Gamma`]($mathtex),
we will show this by induction on the distance between [`u`]($mathtex) and [`v`]($mathtex).
More precisely, given any geodesic edge path from [`u`]($mathtex) to [`v`]($mathtex),
we show that the subset of [`U \cap V`]($mathtex)
which additionally stabilizes this path pointwise belongs to [`\mathcal{B}`]($mathtex),
and is in fact an identity neighborhood in both [`G_u`]($mathtex)
and [`G_v`]($mathtex).

The base case where distance [`0`]($mathtex) (i.e. [`u = v`]($mathtex)) holds because
[`G_v`]($mathtex) is a topological group.

when [`u`]($mathtex) and [`v`]($mathtex) are connected by an edge [`e`]($mathtex),
the intersection [`U \cap V`]($mathtex) is contained in the edge stabilizer [`G_e`]($mathtex).

Suppose now that the result holds for all pairs [`U`]($mathtex) and [`V`]($mathtex)
at distance at most [`n`]($mathtex), we prove the statement for vertices at distance [`n + 1`]($mathtex).
Let [`e_0 \ldots e_n`]($mathtex) be a geodesic edge path from [`u`]($mathtex) to [`v`]($mathtex),
and let [`w`]($mathtex) be the initial vertex of [`e_n`]($mathtex).

By induction, the subset of the intersection [`U \cap G_w`]($mathtex)
which stabilizes the (possibly trivial) path [`e_0 \ldots e_{n-1}`]($mathtex)
is an identity neighborhood in both [`G_u`]($mathtex) and [`G_w`]($mathtex).
Call this subset [`W`]($mathtex).

Since [`G_{e_n}`]($mathtex) inherits the same topology as an open subgroup of each
of [`G_v`]($mathtex) and [`G_w`]($mathtex),
we see that [`V \cap W \subset G_{e_n}`]($mathtex) is an open identity neighborhood
in both [`G_v`]($mathtex) and [`G_w`]($mathtex).
The subset of [`U \cap V`]($mathtex)
stabilizing [`e_0 \ldots e_n`]($mathtex) is clearly contained in [`V \cap W`]($mathtex),
and the reverse is also true: if [`g \in V \cap W`]($mathtex),
then by assumption [`g \in U \cap V`]($mathtex) stabilizes the path [`e_0 \ldots e_{n-1}`]($mathtex)
and also [`v`]($mathtex), whence since [`\Gamma`]($mathtex)
is simplicial, it stabilizes the whole path.

Finally, to see that this topology [`\tau`]($mathtex) is the finest group topology on [`G`]($mathtex)
such that each inclusion [`G_v \to G`]($mathtex) is an open embedding,
observe that any group topology [`\tau'`]($mathtex) satisfying this latter hypothesis
actually makes each [`U \in \mathcal{B}`]($mathtex) into an identity neighborhood,
so the identity map [`(G,\tau') \to (G,\tau)`]($mathtex) is continuous, as required.
[`\QED`]($mathtex).

**Proposition 2** has an immediate consequence for certain (2-categorical) colimits
in the category of topological groups.

> **Corollary 3.**
> Suppose [`\mathcal{G}`]($mathtex) is a connected graph of topological groups
> such that each edge-to-vertex group homomorphism is an open embedding.
> The fundamental group of the graph of groups [`\pi_1(\mathcal{G})`]($mathtex)
> is canonically a topological group. [`\QED`]($mathtex)

One limitation of **Corollary 3** is that it is not strong enough to topologize
some of the most familiar tree-like constructions on groups.
For example, I'll close by leaving you to ponder the following.

> **Exercise 4.** Suppose [`G_1,\ldots,G_n`]($mathtex) are groups.
> The only topology on their free product [`G_1 * \cdots * G_n`]($mathtex)
> which makes the action on the natural Bass-Serre tree associated to the free product
> continuous is the discrete topology. [`\QED`]($mathtex)
